# Exact mixed-monomer interface regression

This note records a local geometric no-go result for the Target A scaffold. It
does not assert that a large mixed phase is globally realizable; it shows that
local cell geometry cannot, by itself, force a single monomer orientation.

Use the triangular-lattice basis \((e_1,e_2)\) with

\[
|a e_1+b e_2|^2=a^2+ab+b^2.
\]

Consider the two edge-sharing elementary triangles

\[
T_+=\{(0,0),(1,0),(0,1)\},
\qquad
T_-=\{(1,0),(0,1),(1,1)\}.
\]

For a rational parameter \(0<t<1/2\), put

\[
p_t=(t,t),
\qquad
q_t=(1-t,1-t).
\]

The barycentric coordinates of \(p_t\) in \(T_+\) are

\[
(1-2t,t,t),
\]

and those of \(q_t\) in \(T_-\) are

\[
(t,t,1-2t).
\]

Thus both blockers are strictly inside their respective cells. Directly from
the quadratic form, the largest squared distance from either blocker to one of
the three vertices of its own triangle is

\[
1-3t+3t^2<1.
\tag{1}
\]

The blockers themselves have squared separation

\[
|p_t-q_t|^2=3(1-2t)^2.
\tag{2}
\]

Therefore this explicit pair is unit-separated whenever

\[
3(1-2t)^2\ge1.
\tag{3}
\]

For the exact regression value \(t=1/10\), the two relevant margins are

\[
\max_{v\in T_+}|p_t-v|^2
=\max_{v\in T_-}|q_t-v|^2
=\frac{73}{100}<1,
\]

and

\[
|p_t-q_t|^2=\frac{48}{25}>1.
\]

## The full conflict neighborhoods

The example is not merely a check against the three displayed vertices. Translate
the origin to the nearby lattice anchor. The lattice vectors with squared norm
at most three are finite, and exact evaluation gives

\[
N_\Lambda(p_{1/10})
=\{(0,0),(1,0),(0,1)\},
\tag{4}
\]

and

\[
N_\Lambda(q_{1/10})
=\{(1,0),(0,1),(1,1)\}.
\tag{5}
\]

No vector outside this shell can be a strict conflict. Indeed, a lattice vector
of squared norm at least four has norm at least two, whereas each blocker is at
oblique distance \(\sqrt3/10<\sqrt3/2\) from its anchor. The reverse triangle
inequality gives a distance larger than one. Consequently (4)--(5) are complete
strict conflict neighborhoods, not truncated neighborhoods.

The two cells share the edge \([(1,0),(0,1)]\), have disjoint interiors, and
their blockers are at distance greater than one. Hence adjacent opposite-
orientation monomers can occur in a locally valid blocker shore with the exact
degree-three neighborhoods required by the scaffold classification.

## An interface of arbitrary finite length

The local example can be assembled along a diagonal. In the index square
\(\{0,\ldots,L-1\}^2\), use

\[
p_{ij}=(i+t,j+t)\quad\text{for }i+j\le L-1,
\]

in the \(+\)-triangle \(T_+(i,j)\), and

\[
q_{ij}=(i+1-t,j+1-t)\quad\text{for }i+j\ge L-1,
\]

in the \(-\)-triangle \(T_-(i,j)\). There are

\[
\frac{L(L+1)}2+\frac{L(L+1)}2=L(L+1)
\tag{6}
\]

blockers. The two index regions meet along a diagonal of \(L\) cells, so this
is a mixed interface whose length grows without bound while the two phases each
occupy a macroscopic triangular region.

Same-orientation blockers differ by a nonzero lattice vector and are therefore
at distance at least one. For a plus blocker and a minus blocker, write

\[
a=k-i+1,\qquad b=\ell-j+1,\qquad r=2t.
\]

The index inequalities imply \(a+b\ge2\), and their difference vector is
\((a-r)e_1+(b-r)e_2\). Since

\[
u^2+uv+v^2
=\frac34(u+v)^2+\frac14(u-v)^2,
\]

we obtain

\[
\begin{aligned}
|q_{k\ell}-p_{ij}|^2
&\ge \frac34(2-2r)^2\\
&=3(1-2t)^2.
\end{aligned}
\tag{7}
\]

Thus the complete diagonal family is unit-separated whenever
\(3(1-2t)^2\ge1\). For example, \(t=1/10\) gives the strict lower bound
\(48/25\) on all cross-orientation distances. The same finite-shell argument
used for the two-cell example translates to every blocker, so each point has
exactly its declared three lattice conflicts; the interface does not introduce
hidden degree-four cells.

The executable certificate checks sides through \(L=11\), but inequality (7) is
independent of \(L\), so the construction is a finite family of arbitrary length,
not a search artifact.

The same family has an explicit incidence matching. Match each \(+\)-cell to
its lower-left vertex \((i,j)\), and each \(-\)-cell to its upper-right vertex
\((i+1,j+1)\). The plus assignments have coordinate sum at most \(L-1\), while
the minus assignments have coordinate sum at least \(L+1\); within each class the
index pair is unique. Thus all \(L(L+1)\) cells are matched to distinct lattice
vertices. The selected vertex set has \((L+1)^2\) sites, so the raw incidence
deficiency is

\[
L(L+1)-(L+1)^2=-(L+1),
\tag{8}
\]

and the left Hall nullity is exactly zero. This is an explicit mixed-interface
family with arbitrary interface length, exact blocker separation, complete local
neighborhoods, and no scaffold nullity generated by the interface itself.

There is also a parameter-free way to see the Hall-neutrality of this corner-well
family. Call a blocker *central* if it has no lattice conflict vertex at distance
less than \(1/2\). Every noncentral blocker can be assigned a conflict vertex at
distance less than \(1/2\). Two unit-separated blockers cannot receive the same
assignment, since the triangle inequality would make their mutual distance less
than one. Hence for any selected cell set \(S\),

\[
|N(S)|\ge |S\setminus C|,
\qquad
|S|-|N(S)|\le |S\cap C|,
\tag{9}
\]

where \(C\) is the set of central blockers. In particular, a positive Hall core
requires central blockers, and its deficiency is at most their number. In the
diagonal construction the anchor distance squared is \(3t^2\); for the certified
range \(t=1/10\) (and more generally \(3t^2<1/4\)), every blocker is noncentral.
The explicit matching above is therefore also an instance of this general
sub-half-anchor bound.

## Frontier consequence

This closes a tempting but invalid shortcut:

> disjoint monomer cells plus unit separation of their blockers force a single
> orientation phase.

They do not. Any phase-rigidity proof must add a genuinely bulk or interface
argument—for example, a quantitative bound on how many such interfaces can be
assembled, or a source-specific diameter variation. The diagonal construction
shows that an interface can be assembled for arbitrary finite length, although it
separates two-dimensional \(+\) and \(-\) regions, and its explicit matching shows
that the interface need not carry Hall nullity. It also does not prove that
this finite scaffold is a globally optimal packing; it only prevents local cell
geometry or interface length from being used as the missing Target A theorem.

The exact arithmetic implementation and regression are in
research/mixed_monomer_interface.py and
research/test_mixed_monomer_interface.py.

**LEVEL I STATUS: STILL OPEN. TARGET A REMAINS UNRESOLVED.**
